                     Case Statements

--> Case Statements checks a variable against multiple patterns.
--> when a pattern matches, the curresponding commands are executed
Syntax:

case variable in
pattern1)
        commands
        ;;
patter2)
        commands
        ;;
pattern3)
        commands
        ;;
*)
   deafult_commands
        ;;
esac

here,
case : start the case Statement
variable : value to check
pattern) : pattern to matches
;; : Ends the current case
* : matches the value
   (default case)
esac : ends the case Statement (esac is case written backword) 

                   **Select Statement**

--> The select statement in bash is used to create menu driven program.
--> it displays a list of options.
syntax:
select variable in option1 option2 option3
do
      commands
done

PS3 : 
--> It is a special bash variable used with select statement
--> it defines the msg that bash displays when it asks the user to select an option.
